<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Monemvasia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Malvasia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Monemvasia is a fortified town on a rocky island off the south-eastern coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, joined to the mainland by a causeway. Founded in the 6th century, it was a strong Byzantine port that fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II of Villehardouin</a> in 1248 and was returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> in 1262. It later belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and under its Italian name, Malvasia, it gave its name to malmsey wine.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monemvasia.jpg" alt="Monemvasia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Katsikas pantelis · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%9A%CE%B1%CF%83%CF%84%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%80%CE%BF%CE%BB%CE%B9%CF%84%CE%B5%CE%B9%CE%B1_%CE%BC%CE%BF%CE%BD%CE%B5%CE%BC%CE%B2%CE%B1%CF%83%CE%B9%CE%B1%CF%82.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Monemvasia stands on a tied island off the east coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, connected to the mainland by a sandy isthmus about 400 metres long. An earthquake in 375 CE separated the rock from the mainland. Most of the rock is a plateau about 100 metres above the sea. The upper town stood on the plateau, with a rectangular acropolis with four towers built in the sixth century, and the lower town lies on the southern slope. There are no natural springs, so the town depended on cisterns.</p><p>The name comes from two Greek words, moni, 'single', and emvasis, 'approach', meaning the city of the single entrance. Its Italian form, Malvasia, gave its name to malmsey wine, which was not grown locally but reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> through the town's extensive trade, mostly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a>, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinos">Tinos</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Monemvasia was founded in the sixth century by settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, then called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Lacedaemon</a>. A later local chronicle says that the city's inhabitants fled a Slav raid in 587 to 588, but archaeology does not support this, since the first level of the church of Christ Elkomenos dates from Justinian's reign, some decades earlier. While other settlements in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> declined from the seventh century, Monemvasia grew into a commercial and cultural centre on sea routes such as the one to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> took most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> in 1460 and 1461, Monemvasia fell to the Venetians by the end of 1463. In the Sixth Ottoman-Venetian War Francesco Morosini failed to take it in 1687 and 1689, but Girolamo Corner entered it on 12 August 1690. In the Greek War of Independence the fortress was besieged from 15 March 1821 and surrendered to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> on 23 July after four months. The war left it nearly ruined, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1828 counted only 659 inhabitants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The upper town has been uninhabited since the second Venetian occupation, but its Byzantine church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>, built on a cross-in-square plan in the style of the twelfth century, survives, with parts of frescoes. A former mosque in Elkomenos square has housed the town's archaeological collection since 1999. From the 1970s Monemvasia revived as a tourist destination, as visitors bought and restored houses, and a bridge linked it to the road network in 1971.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Monemvasia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Geoffrey’s younger son William II of Villehardouin completed the conquest by taking <b>Monemvasia</b> in 1248 after a long siege, and built the fortresses of Mistras, Grand Magne and Beaufort to control the Mani and the Slavic Melingoi of the Taygetus range.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After William II was captured at Pelagonia in 1259, he ceded Mistras, <b>Monemvasia</b> and Grand Magne to Byzantium, and Passavant was lost to the Byzantines soon after, around 1263.</blockquote><a href="/state/barony-of-passavant">Barony of Passavant</a></li><li><blockquote>Byzantium regained Mistras, <b>Monemvasia</b> and the Mani in 1262 from the Principality of Achaea and slowly expanded from there.</blockquote><a href="/state/despotate-of-the-morea">Despotate of the Morea</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Monemvasia</b>, a fortified town on a rock island off the Laconian coast, belonged to the Despotate of the Morea.</blockquote><a href="/state/venetian-monemvasia">Venetian Monemvasia</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Ottoman-Venetian war of 1537-1540, Venice surrendered Nauplia and <b>Monemvasia</b> to the Ottomans under the peace treaty of 1540.</blockquote><a href="/state/venetian-nauplia">Venetian Nauplia</a></li></ul></section>
